1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,571
Coach: A follow-up can change what the listener needs without changing what happened.

2
00:00:05,571 --> 00:00:10,714
Coach: Keep the same facts, then select the part that answers this question.

3
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:17,571
Coach: You do not need a fresh story for every question, or the whole story every time.

4
00:00:17,921 --> 00:00:23,064
Coach: For the visitor-guide case, compare these questions: What did you contribute?

5
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:25,635
Coach: What would you change next time?

6
00:00:25,635 --> 00:00:28,207
Coach: How much did the guide help?

7
00:00:28,207 --> 00:00:33,350
Coach: They ask for an action, a proposed improvement, and evidence of impact.

8
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,064
Coach: Those are different requests.

9
00:00:43,414 --> 00:00:46,414
Coach: For contribution, bring forward drawing the map,

10
00:00:46,414 --> 00:00:49,843
Coach: checking the conflicting names, and changing its label.

11
00:00:49,843 --> 00:00:53,271
Coach: Keep Maya and Noah beside their own work.

12
00:00:53,271 --> 00:01:00,557
Coach: Shortening the account should not turn a shared task into a claim that one person did everything.

13
00:01:00,907 --> 00:01:06,050
You: Next time, I would agree a shared list of room names earlier.

14
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:11,621
You: That could help us check the map and schedule before assembling the guide,

15
00:01:11,621 --> 00:01:15,050
You: rather than noticing the mismatch the day before.

16
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,971
Coach: That answer stays in the future.

17
00:01:17,971 --> 00:01:21,400
Coach: It connects the proposed change to the mismatch,

18
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,828
Coach: without claiming the new process has already worked.

19
00:01:24,828 --> 00:01:32,114
Coach: Do not quietly move a good idea from the reflection card into the history of the event.

20
00:01:32,464 --> 00:01:37,178
You: I saw one visitor use the map to find Blue Room.

21
00:01:37,178 --> 00:01:41,035
You: We did not collect a total or survey visitors,

22
00:01:41,035 --> 00:01:45,321
You: so I cannot say how much the guide helped overall.

23
00:01:45,671 --> 00:01:49,528
Coach: This answers the impact question with the evidence available.

24
00:01:49,528 --> 00:01:55,957
Coach: It does not prove that changing the label caused that visitor to find the room.

25
00:01:55,957 --> 00:01:59,385
Coach: A precise limit can make an account clearer

26
00:01:59,385 --> 00:02:02,814
Coach: than a confident statement the facts cannot support.

27
00:02:11,164 --> 00:02:16,307
Coach: If you prepared your own account, try one different follow-up now.

28
00:02:16,307 --> 00:02:19,307
Coach: Select relevant details you can honestly describe;

29
00:02:19,307 --> 00:02:21,878
Coach: leave other details available for later.

30
00:02:21,878 --> 00:02:27,450
Coach: If you are using the fictional case for language practice, keep that label.

31
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:32,593
Coach: A model gives you a way to practise, not a personal history.

32
00:02:32,943 --> 00:02:35,943
Coach: Before answering, notice what is being asked.

33
00:02:35,943 --> 00:02:38,086
Coach: Then make your contribution, reasoning,

34
00:02:38,086 --> 00:02:41,086
Coach: evidence and uncertainty clear where they matter.

35
00:02:41,086 --> 00:02:47,514
Coach: You can pause, clarify a question, or say that you do not remember a detail.

36
00:02:47,514 --> 00:02:51,800
Coach: The aim is an understandable account you can stand behind.
